WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.520
Professor Terence
Tao is the equivalent

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.665
of a rock star in mathematics.

00:00:04.665 --> 00:00:07.680
 Regarded as the greatest
mathematician alive.

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:13.460
 As long as I can
remember, I have always

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.400
loved numbers and
puzzles and patterns.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.410
Sometimes, because of
the way it's taught,

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.720
innate mathematical ability
doesn't feel connected

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.460
to the way we learn math.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.490
But I think we all have an
innate ability for mathematics.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.560
Knowing how to
think mathematically

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.409
can be a tool that you
can use to solve problems

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:33.760
in a systematic way.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.010
I like to think that this
class is for anyone, really.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.968
In fact, maybe this class
is even more suitable

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:40.390
for people who have not had
formal mathematical training.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.848
I'm not going to
give you homework.

00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:43.930
The way I approached
problems in mathematics

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.773
was to try to turn
it into a game.

00:00:46.773 --> 00:00:48.190
A large part of
this class will be

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:49.720
about talking about
problem solving.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.290
We abstract them.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:52.700
We break them up into pieces.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.770
We make analogies.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.670
We try to find connections
with other problems.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:01.420
The first player who can collect
three numbers that add up to 15

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.387
wins the game.

00:01:02.387 --> 00:01:03.970
Playing this game
is quite challenging

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.137
if you don't know the trick.

00:01:05.137 --> 00:01:07.900
You can transform this to
a much more familiar game,

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:08.920
tic-tac-toe.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.312
People often anthropomorphize
mathematical objects.

00:01:11.312 --> 00:01:12.520
They call this guy the enemy.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.037
This guy's your friend.

00:01:14.037 --> 00:01:16.620
Some people call them slightly
more obscene names, okay, but--

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.800
[LAUGHS]

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.710
In this class, you'll appreciate
a little bit more what

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.080
mathematics is, how
we solve problems,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.260
and how some of these
strategies can be of use to you

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.610
in your daily life.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.320
The ideal problem
to work on is one

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.937
which is just barely
outside of your reach.

00:01:33.937 --> 00:01:35.770
It's not so much whether
you succeed or fail

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:36.437
at the question.

00:01:36.437 --> 00:01:39.190
But it's whether you can
learn something from it.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.470
My name is Terence Tao,
and this is MasterClass.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

